      "status": "mathematical proof reconstructed",
      "correspondence": "The dual of Q injects into the invariant class characters, identified by Artin pullback with E. All elements of E factor through U/N, and all characters of U/N belong to E. A finite-group count compares these with characters of G_F/N. Inflation sends the latter into the finite base-field character space with ramification allowed over ℓ. The C₉ example distinguishes a cardinality comparison from extendability of individual characters.",
      "review": "Checked the ramification sets and the role of the primitive root and archimedean hypothesis. Enumerated the fixed line, coinvariants and invariant dual in the finite unipotent model. An independent reader confirmed the comparison and separated finiteness of G/N from its commutativity."
    },
    {
      "id": "ACI-02",
      "chapter": "inputs",
      "title": "The index and the Stickelberger exponent have separate roles in the class-rank bound",
      "anchor": "inputs-ambiguous-class-tower",
      "statement": "Fix (f,ℓ)=1, a=1 for odd ℓ or 2 for ℓ=2, F=ℚ(μ_{fℓ^a}), K=ℚ(μ_{fℓ^n}), n≥2. Relative class coinvariants attached to γ≡1+fℓ^a mod fℓ^n have size ≤B_F. When f>1, a separate nonvanishing input supplies N and n₀, and integral Stickelberger annihilation gives |(1−J)C ∩ C[ℓ]|≤|Q|^N≤B_F^N for n≥n₀.",
      "hypotheses": "On the cyclotomic class group γ acts through the inverse of its Galois automorphism. The group-ring exponent is uniform for fixed ℓ,f,c=2fℓ+1. The mod-ℓ nonvanishing tests the twisted projection for characters satisfying χ(−1)=−1. The final two-primary estimate adds a signature bound, and the odd-prime estimate uses reflection.",
      "definitions": [
        "M=fℓ^n and the kernel generated by γ has index φ(fℓ^a) in (ℤ/M)×. This index is distinct from [C:ℓC+(1−τ)C].",
        "J denotes complex conjugation. The group-ring argument uses the integral operator Θ(1−J) and the ideal-membership assertion (γ−1)^N∈(Θ,1+J,ℓ).",
        "With f=2, ℓ=3 and n=2, the three quantities [units:⟨γ⟩], |Q| and B_F are respectively 2, 1 and 9."
      ],
